### What is the climbing stairs problem?

You are climbing a staircase with N steps. Each move takes either one step or two steps. The question asks how many distinct sequences of moves reach the top. This is one of the most frequently asked dynamic programming questions in interviews.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ClimbingStairs {

    public static long countWays(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return 1;
        long prev2 = 1, prev1 = 1;  // dp[0] = 1, dp[1] = 1
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            long current = prev1 + prev2;
            prev2 = prev1;
            prev1 = current;
        }
        return prev1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int n = 0; n <= 10; n++) {
            System.out.println("climb(" + n + ") = " + countWays(n));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
climb(0) = 1
climb(1) = 1
climb(2) = 2
climb(3) = 3
climb(4) = 5
climb(5) = 8
climb(6) = 13
climb(7) = 21
climb(8) = 34
climb(9) = 55
climb(10) = 89
```

### Why is the answer a Fibonacci number?

To reach step N, the last move was either a one-step from step N-1 or a two-step from step N-2. These two groups of sequences are completely separate because they end with different moves. So the count at step N is dp[N-1] + dp[N-2], which is the Fibonacci recurrence.

With base cases dp[0] = 1 and dp[1] = 1, the sequence is 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21... This matches the Fibonacci sequence shifted by one position: climb(N) = fib(N+1).

### How does the bottom-up rolling approach work?

Since each value depends only on the previous two, we keep two variables and iterate forward. This gives O(n) time and O(1) space, which is optimal.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ClimbingStairsRolling {

    public static long climb(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return 1;
        long twoBelow = 1;  // dp[i-2]
        long oneBelow = 1;  // dp[i-1]
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            long current = oneBelow + twoBelow;
            twoBelow = oneBelow;
            oneBelow = current;
        }
        return oneBelow;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("climb(4) = " + climb(4));
        System.out.println("climb(5) = " + climb(5));
    }
}
```

```text
climb(4) = 5
climb(5) = 8
```

### How is this different from the three-step staircase?

| Aspect | Two-step (1 or 2) | Three-step (1, 2, or 3) |
|---|---|---|
| Recurrence | dp[n-1] + dp[n-2] | dp[n-1] + dp[n-2] + dp[n-3] |
| Rolling variables | 2 | 3 |
| Base cases | dp[0]=1, dp[1]=1 | dp[0]=1, dp[1]=1, dp[2]=2 |
| Sequence | Fibonacci | Tribonacci |
| climb(5) | 8 | 13 |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class TwoVsThreeStep {

    public static long twoStep(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return 1;
        long a = 1, b = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) { long c = a + b; a = b; b = c; }
        return b;
    }

    public static long threeStep(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return 1;
        if (n == 2) return 2;
        long a = 1, b = 1, c = 2;
        for (int i = 3; i <= n; i++) { long d = a + b + c; a = b; b = c; c = d; }
        return c;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Two-step climb(5):   " + twoStep(5));
        System.out.println("Three-step climb(5): " + threeStep(5));
    }
}
```

```text
Two-step climb(5):   8
Three-step climb(5): 13
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is setting dp[0] to 0 instead of 1. dp[0] = 1 represents the fact that standing at the bottom without moving is one valid way. If dp[0] = 0, the two-step move from step 0 to step 2 is not counted.

```java
// WRONG: dp[0] = 0 means a two-step jump to step 2 is not counted
long a = 0, b = 1;  // dp[0] = 0, dp[1] = 1
// climb(2) = 0 + 1 = 1 (should be 2: 1+1 or 2)

// CORRECT: dp[0] = 1 counts standing still as a valid state
long a = 1, b = 1;  // dp[0] = 1, dp[1] = 1
// climb(2) = 1 + 1 = 2
```

### What are the edge cases?

When N is zero, there is one way (do nothing). When N is one, there is one way (a single step). When N is negative, there are zero ways.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ClimbEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("climb(0) = " + ClimbingStairs.countWays(0));  // 1
        System.out.println("climb(1) = " + ClimbingStairs.countWays(1));  // 1
        System.out.println("climb(2) = " + ClimbingStairs.countWays(2));  // 2
    }
}
```

```text
climb(0) = 1
climb(1) = 1
climb(2) = 2
```

### How does this generalise to K step sizes?

If the allowed step sizes are given as a set rather than fixed at {1, 2}, the recurrence sums over all allowed steps. This becomes the permutation-style coin-change count where order matters.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class GeneralStaircase {

    public static long countWays(int n, int[] steps) {
        long[] dp = new long[n + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int step : steps) {
                if (step <= i) dp[i] += dp[i - step];
            }
        }
        return dp[n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Steps {1,2}, n=5: " + countWays(5, new int[]{1, 2}));     // 8
        System.out.println("Steps {2,3}, n=6: " + countWays(6, new int[]{2, 3}));     // 4
    }
}
```

```text
Steps {1,2}, n=5: 8
Steps {2,3}, n=6: 4
```

### When should you use this in banking?

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh modelled a customer onboarding flow where each action advanced by one or two steps. The product team needed the count of distinct action sequences to understand flexibility and to size their audit-trail database. The rolling Fibonacci approach computed results instantly.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class OnboardingPathCounter {

    public static long countPaths(int steps) {
        if (steps <= 1) return 1;
        long a = 1, b = 1;
        for (int i = 2; i <= steps; i++) {
            long c = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }
        return b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Paths for 5-step onboarding: " + countPaths(5));
        System.out.println("Paths for 10-step onboarding: " + countPaths(10));
    }
}
```

```text
Paths for 5-step onboarding: 8
Paths for 10-step onboarding: 89
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognise that climbing stairs with steps of one or two is exactly the Fibonacci sequence. The insight is that the last move partitions the sequences into two non-overlapping groups, and summing them gives the recurrence.

This problem is often the gateway to more complex DP questions. Interviewers use it to check whether a candidate can identify the Fibonacci structure, optimise space to O(1), and generalise to K step sizes. Being able to state that climb(N) = fib(N+1) with the standard Fibonacci indexing shows real understanding.
